-We introduce Nash Bargaining Preference Optimization (\NBPO{}) for this setting. Within each objective, \NBPO{} evaluates the learner against an adaptive opponent that emphasizes responses exposing the learner's weaknesses, while a KL penalty keeps the opponent near the reference policy. Across objectives, it maximizes the Nash product of the resulting improvements over that same reference. The standard bargaining properties are useful in LLM alignment because they ensure that no objective falls below the fallback at the population optimum, no other feasible policy can improve one objective without worsening another, and arbitrary judge-specific units do not alter the selected compromise.
-
-The main technical challenge is optimization, not re-deriving the classical bargaining axioms. Three issues arise simultaneously: the reference policy has zero improvement and lies on the boundary of the logarithmic objective; each adaptive opponent depends on the policy being optimized; and the ideal exponential policy update contains an intractable normalizing constant. We address these issues with a constrained KL-proximal formulation, closed-form opponent reweighting, a low-dimensional dual update whose weights are inversely proportional to achieved improvements, and partition-free pairwise log-ratio regression from binary comparisons.
+Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) commonly learns scalar rewards from pairwise comparisons \citep{christiano2017deep,ouyang2022training}. Direct Preference Optimization removes the separate reward model but retains a Bradley--Terry (BT) preference model \citep{rafailov2023direct,bradley1952rank}. For a fixed prompt and objective, BT represents population probabilities as $P_k(y\succ z\mid x)=\sigma(r_k(x,y)-r_k(x,z))$. This permits noisy, contradictory sampled labels, but cannot represent a cyclic strict majority relation.
+
+For example, under one fixed rubric, suppose $P_k(A\succ B)=P_k(B\succ C)=P_k(C\succ A)=0.75$. No scalar scores can represent these three probabilities. This hypothetical within-rubric cycle differs from comparing relevance on one edge and safety on another. Whether such misspecification remains after defining separate objectives is an empirical question; sparse human comparison graphs and scalar-rating-induced labels do not establish it.
+
+Nash Learning from Human Feedback (NLHF) retains general pairwise payoffs, and INPO, SPPO, and related methods provide scalable equilibrium-seeking updates \citep{munos2024nash,zhang2025iterative,wu2025sppo,calandriello2024online,rosset2024direct,zhou2025egpo}. Multiple objectives additionally require a compromise rule. Existing methods use scalarization, constraints, policy interpolation, preference conditioning, or Blackwell criteria \citep{zhou2024beyond,rame2023rewarded,zhong2024panacea,agnihotri2026mopo,zhang2026prosper}.
+
+We use classical Nash bargaining with equal bargaining weight for the specified objectives \citep{nash1950}. It maximizes their surplus product over a common fallback without user-specified scalarization weights. Equal treatment is a choice: duplicating or splitting an objective changes its influence, and the fallback affects the result. Prior work already studies disagreement-adjusted Nash welfare over learned party rewards and bargaining over task-gradient improvements \citep{zhong2024nashwelfare,navon2022nashmtl}.
+
+Nash Bargaining Preference Optimization (\NBPO{}) instead bargains over reference-relative \emph{policy game values}. Each objective evaluates the learner against an adaptive, KL-regularized comparator. Improvement means a larger robustness-adjusted game value, not necessarily a direct win over the reference. Population bargaining guarantees concern these specified utilities and do not imply per-prompt improvement.
+
+The technical difficulty is the coupled proximal problem: surplus is zero at the reference, and each opponent depends on the policy being optimized. Pairwise normalizer cancellation and regression fitting are inherited tools \citep{zhang2025iterative,zhang2026prosper}. Our optimizer derives the surplus dual, solves its prompt-separable finite-pool subproblems directly, and then fits their target to an LLM. This replaces an undamped best-response alternation that can be unstable even when its fixed point is optimal. Numerical target accuracy, neural realization, and independent policy quality are evaluated separately.
 
