+The following Bregman proximal-point guarantee concerns exact population solves, not finite-pool fitting or empirical acceptance; $T$ counts exact solves rather than neural updates. Under Assumption~\ref{ass:joint}, with $\pi_0=\piref$ and \eqref{eq:prox-subproblem} solved exactly at every outer iteration with arbitrary $\eta_t>0$, the Nash welfare is monotone, $F(\pi_{t+1})\ge F(\pi_t)$ for $t\ge1$, and $F(\pi^\star)-F(\pi_T)\le D(\pi^\star\|\piref)/\sum_{t<T}\eta_t$: a constant proximal step gives an $O(1/T)$ final-iterate gap. Theorem~\ref{thm:last-iterate} and its proof are in Appendix~\ref{app:convergence}.
 
