-Several limitations remain. The current component ablation does not establish an empirical benefit from adaptive game values over the fixed-reference Nash variant. The neural algorithm also approximates the policy--opponent fixed point, dual solution, and regression target from finite samples, whereas the convergence theorem assumes exact population updates. Finally, the headline cross-method comparison must be completed under common independent metrics, reward-model baselines, and multiple training seeds. The joint-improvement assumption can also fail when no policy improves every objective over the chosen reference; in that case a positive-surplus Nash solution does not exist. Improvements in game value should not be interpreted as per-prompt safety guarantees.
+\NBPO{} combines adaptive preference-game values with Nash bargaining over reference-relative improvements. A constrained proximal formulation yields a direct finite-pool solver and an $O(1/T)$ exact-population guarantee. Controlled experiments support adaptive game representations. Three policy seeds reproduce teacher-defined gains on SafeRLHF without a resolved advantage over mass-matched utilitarian aggregation. Broader instruction and independent objective comparisons remain unmeasured. Teacher truncation, refusal-concentrated gains, and development-informed schedules limit the current evidence; empirical Pareto optimality is not established. Joint improvement need not be feasible, and population game values provide no per-prompt safety guarantee.
 
