+\paragraph{Common evaluation and mechanism comparison.}
+Within a panel, all policies use the same frozen evaluator, disagreement construction, comparator bank, and evaluation temperatures, regardless of training temperatures. The planned mechanism comparison reuses fresh generations from the reference, NBPO, fixed-reference Nash, and one development-selected competitor on a fixed held-out subset. Independently judged cross-play and common-temperature finite-bank game values complement direct-reference win rates. Comparator selection is frozen on development data; this comparison remains unmeasured.
+
+\paragraph{Feedback budgets and provenance.}
+We record unique semantic comparisons separately from order-swapped calls, generated tokens, preference-model fitting/scoring, solver time, neural training, and tuning trials. Numerical iterations reuse cached matrices and do not supply new labels. Budget-matched claims require a shared feedback contract and total-compute accounting. All responses, derived pairs, and reversed orders from one source prompt remain in the same split. Manifests identify feedback source, teacher/rubric revisions, objective order, split, comparator construction, and decoding. Historical pair-split, precursor, or differently judged results are not pooled with the repaired panel. Instruction-following error types, length, and refusal patterns are descriptive diagnostics; output-defined subgroups alone do not identify a causal mechanism.
+
+\subsection{Empirical Pareto evaluation}
+\label{app:pareto-evaluation}
+The population Nash optimum is Pareto efficient under the stated assumptions; a single proximal step and approximate neural realization do not inherit this certificate. The planned instruction comparison evaluates all four attributes using a fixed independent local judge. A policy is empirically nondominated only relative to the evaluated policies and evaluator in this four-dimensional space. The two main-figure projections are not complete frontiers.
+
+Scalarized DPO \citep{rafailov2023direct} uses scalar BT rewards calibrated on development data. The declared weight set contains the four coordinate vectors, $(.25,.25,.25,.25)$, $(.1,.1,.1,.7)$, and $(.1,.7,.1,.1)$, in IF/TF/Hon/Help order. Its main-table row denotes the uniform setting; all weight settings appear in the trade-off data. Actual MOPO and PROSPER adaptations preserve their respective constraints and game updates. All rows are unmeasured until corresponding outputs are available.
+
+Compare methods on the complete final set and prespecified label-disagreement subsets. Keep independent win rates separate from training-teacher game values. Report individual policy seeds, paired prompt uncertainty, and all declared weights. Curves joining points do not represent evaluated mixture policies. Nash welfare is defined only for positive game surpluses; mark other cases infeasible. Fix hypervolume normalization and reference points before final scores are opened.
+
+\paragraph{Uncertainty and provenance.}
+Report every policy seed, then mean and standard deviation across training seeds. Each paired prompt-bootstrap replicate recomputes objective means and then the minimum. Its intervals condition on a checkpoint and do not include training-seed variability. Freeze development choices before confirmatory comparisons and retain the history of benchmark exposure. Nash/utilitarian aggregates use three policy seeds, while the historical WBC/long-schedule snapshots use one. Missing method-wise values must not be reconstructed from ranges pooled across methods; dashes are unmeasured, not zeros. All result updates must record checkpoint, split, decoding, evaluator, sample count, and source artifact. Unmatched literature scores do not fill matched-baseline rows.
